  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 422 AM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nFor most of the upcoming week and into the start of next weekend, \nGeorgia will be between a strong ridge over the south-central US, \nand troughing over the Eastern Seaboard. On Tuesday, a warm and \nhumid airmass will remain parked over the region, allowing high \ntemps to climb into the mid to upper 90s, with heat index values in \nthe 100-108 range. While most of the day looks to be rain-free, some \nof the guidance has a shortwave riding along the top of the ridge \nand dropping across the area late in the day to overnight. Depending \non the timing of this, it could bring a round of strong to severe \nstorms through the area, as we typically see in northwest flow \nregimes. \n\nModel guidance is starting to get into better agreement on a frontal \nboundary also pushing through the area on Wednesday, which would \nallow a drier and relatively cooler airmass to spread over the \nregion through the end of the work week. High temperatures would \nstill be in the low to mid 90s, but dewpoints in the 60s would keep \nheat index values near the ambient temperature. With PWAT values \nlikely falling to around 1 inch, rainfall chances will be limited \nfrom late Wednesday through Friday.",
